SHAP - SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) is a game-theoretic approach used to explain the output of any machine learning model. It breaks down a prediction to show how much each individual feature contributes to that result.

Core Concepts

• Shapley Values: Rooted in cooperative game theory, features act as "players" in a game where the payout is the model's prediction.
• Local vs. Global Interpretations:
	• Local: Explains why a specific prediction was made for a single instance (e.g., why a loan was rejected).
	• Global: Aggregates local values across an entire dataset to show overall feature importance and trends.
• Direction of Impact: SHAP reveals whether a feature pushed the model output higher (positive) or lower (negative).

Common Explainers & Methods

• TreeSHAP: A fast, model-specific method optimized for tree-based models like XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost.
• KernelSHAP: A slower, model-agnostic method that treats the model as a black box and samples feature subsets.
• DeepSHAP / GradientSHAP: Designed for deep learning models using frameworks like TensorFlow, Keras, and PyTorch.

Popular Visualizations

• Waterfall Plot: Shows how individual feature values start from a base value and push the prediction up or down to reach the final output.
• Summary / Beeswarm Plot: Combines global feature importance with the distribution of impact direction across a dataset.
• Bar Plot: Displays the mean absolute impact of each feature.

Installation & Usage

You can install the official Python library via PyPI or conda:
pip install shap
# or
conda install -c conda-forge shap

